<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sexagenarian Law</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1885</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sexagenarian Law was a Brazilian law passed in 1885 that freed enslaved people aged sixty and over. It was one of the gradual abolition measures, following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/law-of-the-free-womb">Law of the Free Womb</a> of 1871 and preceding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lei-aurea">Lei Áurea</a> of 1888. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a> present it as a step in the empire's slow path to abolition.</p></div>
